\C{pageant} Using \i{kageant} for authentication

kageant is an SSH \i{authentication agent}. It holds your \i{private key}s
in memory, already decoded, so that you can use them often
\I{passwordless login}without needing to type a \i{passphrase}.

\H{pageant-start} Getting started with kageant

Before you run kageant, you need to have a private key in \c{*.\i{PPK}}
format. See \k{pubkey} to find out how to generate and use one.

When you run kageant, it will put an icon of a computer wearing a
hat into the \ii{System tray}. It will then sit and do nothing, until you
load a private key into it. (You may need to use Windows'
\q{Show hidden icons} arrow to see the kageant icon.)

If you click the kageant icon with the right mouse button, you will
see a menu. Select \q{Agent Keys & Configuration} from this menu. The kageant main
window will appear. (You can also bring this window up by
double-clicking on the kageant icon.)

The kageant window contains a list box. This shows the private keys
kageant is holding. When you start kageant, it has no keys, so the
list box will be empty. After you add one or more keys, they will
show up in the list box.

To add a key to kageant, press the \q{Add Key} button. kageant will
bring up a file dialog, labelled \q{Select Private Key File}. Find
your private key file in this dialog, and press \q{Open}.

kageant will now load the private key. If the key is protected by a
passphrase, kageant will prompt you to type the passphrase. When the
key has been loaded, it will appear in the list in the kageant
window.

Now start KiTTY++ and open an SSH session to a site that accepts your
key. KiTTY++ will notice that kageant is running, retrieve the key
automatically from kageant, and use it to authenticate. You can now
open as many KiTTY++ sessions as you like without having to type your
passphrase again.

(KiTTY++ can be configured not to try to use kageant, but it will try
by default. See \k{config-ssh-tryagent} and
\k{using-cmdline-agentauth} for more information.)

When you want to shut down kageant, click the right button on the
kageant icon in the System tray, and select \q{Exit} from the menu.
Closing the kageant main window does \e{not} shut down kageant.

If you want kageant to stay running but forget all the keys it has
acquired, right-click the key list in the main window and select
\q{Remove All Keys}.

\S{pageant-tray-sessions} Starting sessions from the tray menu

\q{Quick Connect} in the tray menu opens the KiTTY++ configuration box
on Default Settings, ready for a host (\c{kitty \-quickconnect}).

\q{Saved Sessions} lists the saved sessions as the folder tree: one
submenu per folder, nested as the folders are, sorted by name with
numbers in order. With \c{savemode=dir} it lists the session folder
(\c{sessions=}, \c{fileextension=}) instead of the registry. A session
is started by its full path. The menu lists up to 3584 sessions.

\H{pageant-mainwin} The kageant main window

The kageant main window appears when you left-click on the kageant
system tray icon, or alternatively right-click and select \q{Agent
Keys & Configuration} from the menu. You can use it to keep track of what keys are
currently loaded into kageant, and to add new ones or remove the
existing keys.

\S{pageant-mainwin-keylist} The key list box

The large list box in the kageant main window lists the private keys
that are currently loaded into kageant. The list might look
something like this:

\c Ed25519    SHA256:TddlQk20DVs4LRcAsIfDN9pInKpY06D+h4kSHwWAj4w
\c RSA  2048  SHA256:8DFtyHm3kQihgy52nzX96qMcEVOq7/yJmmwQQhBWYFg

For each key, the list box will show you:

\b The type of the key. Currently, this can be
\q{RSA} (an RSA key for use with the SSH-2 protocol),
\q{DSA} (a DSA key for use with the SSH-2 protocol),
\q{\i{NIST}} (an ECDSA key for use with the SSH-2 protocol),
\q{Ed25519} (an Ed25519 key for use with the SSH-2 protocol),
\q{Ed448} (an Ed448 key for use with the SSH-2 protocol),
or \q{SSH-1} (an RSA key for use with the old SSH-1 protocol).
(If the key has an associated certificate, this is shown here with a
\q{cert} suffix.)

\b The size (in bits) of the key, for key types that come in different
sizes. (For ECDSA \q{NIST} keys, this is indicated as \q{p256} or
\q{p384} or \q{p521}.)

\b The \I{key fingerprint}fingerprint for the public key. This should be
the same fingerprint given by kittygen, and (hopefully) also the same
fingerprint shown by remote utilities such as \i\c{ssh-keygen} when
applied to your \c{authorized_keys} file.

\lcont{
For SSH-2 keys, by default this is shown in the \q{SHA256} format. You
can change to the older \q{MD5} format (which looks like \c{aa:bb:cc:...})
with the \q{Fingerprint type} drop-down, but bear in mind that this
format is less secure and should be avoided for comparison purposes
where possible.

If some of the keys loaded into kageant have certificates attached,
then kageant will default to showing the fingerprint of the underlying
key. This way, a certified and uncertified version of the same key
will have the same fingerprint, so you can see that they match. You
can instead use the \q{Fingerprint type} drop-down to ask for a
different fingerprint to be shown for certified keys, which includes
the certificate as part of the fingerprinted data. That way you can
tell two certificates apart.
}

\b The comment attached to the key.

\b The state of deferred decryption, if enabled for this key.
See \k{pageant-deferred-decryption}.

\S{pageant-mainwin-keydetails} The Key details window

Double-click a key in the key list, or select it and press Enter, to
open \q{kageant - key details}. The fields are read-only text that can
be selected and copied. The window can be resized. A setting changed
here takes effect at once. In window order:

\b \q{Key:} the key type and its size in bits.

\b \q{State:} \q{loaded and ready to use}, encrypted until its first
use, loaded from an encrypted key file, or not loaded with the reason:
the key file is not reachable, the file does not load, the file holds
another key than the one recorded, or no fingerprint is on record for
the path.

\b \q{Fingerprints:} SHA-256 and MD5, for a certified key also both
forms including the certificate. A key not loaded shows the
fingerprint recorded at the last save, if there is one.

\b \q{Comment:} the key's comment.

\b \q{Loaded from:} each key file the key was loaded from, with its
protection: \q{passphrase}, \q{Windows Hello protected} or
\q{UNPROTECTED - no passphrase}. A key not loaded shows its startup
path. A key added by another program shows \q{not known}.
\q{Locate...} appears for a startup entry whose file is not reachable
or does not load. It opens a file picker: a file that holds the
recorded key becomes the entry's new path. A file with another key, or
one that does not load, changes nothing.

\b \q{Destinations:} the key's destination rules: the count on the
first line, then one line per destination. Without rules \q{any - the
key is not restricted}, with a damaged rule file \q{unknown: the rule
file is damaged}. \q{Restrict...} opens the rule editor on this key,
for a loaded SSH-2 key only. See \k{pageant-restrict}.

\b \q{Lifetime:} for a key added with a lifetime (\c{ssh-add -t}) the
time set and the time remaining, counted down while the window is open,
then \q{expired - key removed}. Otherwise \q{unlimited}, or \q{not
loaded}.

\b \q{Load this key deferred at startup (passphrase at first use)}: the
startup list adds the key encrypted at the next start (see
\k{pageant-deferred-decryption}). The stored entry changes at once. The
key's current state stays. Greyed for a key the startup list does not
track and for a key file without a passphrase.

\b \q{Confirm each use:} \q{no}, \q{ask before each use} (a
confirmation box before each signature) or \q{ask with Windows Hello}
(a Windows Hello gesture before each signature). It applies to the
loaded key at once. The startup entry keeps it when the startup list
tracks the key. Greyed for a key not loaded.

\b \q{Re-encrypt after idle:} the key's own idle time (see
\k{pageant-autoencrypt}): \c{immediately}, \c{10m}, \c{2h}, \c{1d} or a
typed time. Blank = the agent default, shown beside the field. The
value applies when a listed value is picked or the field loses the
focus. A time that does not parse restores the previous value. Greyed
for a key not loaded and while the agent setting is \q{Enforced for
every key}.

\b \q{Accept this key}: shown only for an entry refused because the
file holds another key or no fingerprint is on record. Its question
shows the file, the current fingerprint and the recorded one if there
is one. Yes loads the key and records the new fingerprint. With
\q{Confirmations require Windows Hello} on (Settings, \q{Security}
page), Accept also requires a Windows Hello gesture. This is the only
place a changed key file is accepted.

\b \q{Protect with Windows Hello...}: writes a Windows Hello protected
copy of the key file (the copy and its \c{.hello} file, see
\k{ppk-hello}). The original stays where it is. \q{Replace this key's
startup entry with the protected copy} in the protect window puts the
copy in the startup list. Enabled for a loaded key from a file without
a \c{.hello} file, on a computer that offers Windows Hello.

\b \q{Forget a path...}: goes through the key's paths one by one. Yes
removes that path from the startup list, No goes to the next, Cancel
stops. The key stays loaded. Forgetting the key's only startup entry
means it no longer loads at startup. Greyed for a key with no path on
record.

\b \q{Close} (Enter, Esc) closes the window.

F1 opens this section. F1 on \q{Destinations:} or \q{Restrict...} opens
\k{pageant-restrict}.

\S{pageant-mainwin-addkey} The \q{Add Key} button

To add a key to kageant by reading it out of a local disk file,
press the \q{Add Key} button in the kageant main window.

kageant will bring up a file dialog, labelled \q{Select Private Key
File}. Find your private key file in this dialog, and press
\q{Open}. If you want to add more than one key at once, you can
select multiple files using Shift-click (to select several adjacent
files) or Ctrl-click (to select non-adjacent files).

kageant will now load the private key(s). If a key is protected by a
passphrase, kageant will prompt you to type the passphrase.

(This is not the only way to add a private key to kageant. You can
also add one from a remote system by using agent forwarding; see
\k{pageant-forward} for details.)

\S{pageant-mainwin-remkey} The \q{Remove Key} button

If you need to remove a key from kageant, select that key in the
list box, and press the \q{Remove Key} button. kageant will remove
the key from its memory.

You can apply this to keys you added using the \q{Add Key} button,
or to keys you added remotely using agent forwarding (see
\k{pageant-forward}); it makes no difference.

\S{pageant-mainwin-settings} The \q{Settings} button

The \q{Settings} button opens kageant's own settings, on four pages.
Everything here is saved as soon as you press OK; it belongs to this
installation of kageant, not to any key.
With \c{readonly=yes} in kitty.ini the dialog shows \q{Read-only
(readonly=yes): changes apply until kageant exits.} - nothing is
stored, key list changes included.

\b \q{Agent}: whether kageant registers itself as the Windows OpenSSH
agent, whether it uses OpenSSH's \c{known_hosts} as a host-key source
(\q{Use OpenSSH's known_hosts as a host-key source}, off by default,
\k{pageant-knownhosts}), starts at login, reloads the remembered keys at
startup, and shows a notice when a key is used; the notice display time;
the colour theme.

\b \q{Security}: the IPC policy (refuse all key adds and removes over
the pipe, or only adds, or only removes), \q{Allow locking the agent
over IPC}, \q{Refuse key changes from a forwarded agent (add, remove,
lock)} (on by default, \k{pageant-forward-changes}), whether confirmations demand
a Windows Hello gesture, how long a typed passphrase and a Windows
Hello unlock are kept for a batch of keys, and \q{Re-encrypt keys
after idle} - the mode and the time after which an unused key goes
back to its encrypted state (\k{pageant-autoencrypt}).

\b \q{Removable media}: what to do with startup keys whose drive was
absent at login and appears later, whether a key is unloaded when its
media is removed, and whether missing startup keys are reported.

\b \q{Log}: the agent log - whether it is written, where, and how it
is rotated and expunged. It can also go to the Windows Event Log
(Destination): its own log \c{KiTTY++_Agent} when KiTTY++ is installed
for all users, otherwise the Application log under the source
\c{KiTTY++_agent}. Each kind of record has its own event ID and each
field is its own value in the event data, so Event Viewer filters,
Windows Event Forwarding and a SIEM can select on them. Size and
retention are set in Event Viewer or with \c{wevtutil}.

\lcont{
The system-wide installation creates the \c{KiTTY++_Agent} log with a
maximum size of 20 MB; when it is full, the oldest records are
overwritten. To change that, for example to 50 MB:
\c{wevtutil sl KiTTY++_Agent /ms:52428800}. Without that installation
the records go to the Application log, whose size and retention are
Windows' own settings.

100 sign, 101 confirm, 102 session-bind, 103 refused request, 104
refused by a key's destination rules or a key list they left empty, 110 add,
111 load at startup, 112 forget a path, 113 retry, 114 remove, 115
remove all, 116 rules changed in the rule editor, 120 lock, 121 unlock, 130 Hello enrol, 131 Hello protect,
132 Hello unlock, 140 key file warning, 150 re-encrypt all, 151
re-encrypt one key, 152 decrypt a key, 160 agent start/stop, 170 log
rotation. Refusals,
failures and key file warnings are warnings, the rest information. The
fields of each ID, in order, are listed in \k{kitty-eventids}.

Every refused request is a record too: kind \c{request}, result
\c{refused}, the agent's reason (\q{key not found}, \q{unrecognised
message}, \q{key usage not confirmed by user}) and the program that
sent it: event ID 103, a warning.

On a connection whose client sent a verified session-bind, signatures,
confirmations and refused requests carry its destination (\c{dest},
\c{fwd}; see \k{pageant-destination}). The session-bind itself is a
record of kind \c{session-bind}, event ID 102.

OK in the rule editor writes one record of kind \c{rules} for each key
whose rules it changed: result \c{changed}, reason \c{gui}, kageant
itself as the program, and the key's rules now in \c{restrict} (empty
when every rule was removed): event ID 116. \c{kageant -R} writes the
same kind: result \c{removed} or \c{refused}, reason \c{cli}. A key whose rules stayed
the same gets none. Cancel writes none. A rule set with \c{ssh-add
-h} or kageant's \c{-h} is in the \c{restrict} field of the add record
(110).
}

Press F1 on any page for this section; on the idle row it opens the
idle section.

\H{pageant-cmdline} The kageant command line

kageant can be made to do things automatically when it starts up, by
\I{command-line arguments}specifying instructions on its command line.
If you're starting kageant from the Windows GUI, you can arrange this
by editing the properties of the \i{Windows shortcut} that it was
started from.

If kageant is already running, invoking it again with the options
below causes actions to be performed with the existing instance, not a
new one.

\S{pageant-cmdline-loadkey} Making kageant automatically load keys
on startup

kageant can automatically load one or more private keys when it
starts up, if you provide them on the kageant command line. Your
command line might then look like:

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe d:\main.ppk d:\secondary.ppk

If the keys are stored encrypted, kageant will request the
passphrases on startup.

If kageant is already running, this syntax loads keys into the
existing kageant.

You can specify the \c{\-\-encrypted} option to defer decryption of
these keys; see \k{pageant-deferred-decryption}.

\S{pageant-cmdline-restrict} Restricting the keys of the command line:
\c{-h}

\I{-h-kageant}\c{-h} restricts every key file of the command line to a
destination, as OpenSSH's \c{ssh-add -h} does (\k{pageant-restrict}):

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe -h web01 -h "jump01>deploy@web02" d:\main.ppk

The values are OpenSSH's: \c{host}, \c{user@host}, \c{via>host} or
\c{via>user@host}, a host also as \c{host:port} or \c{[host]:port}.
The last host may be a pattern within the rule editor's limit
(\c{*.customer.com}). \c{-h} is repeatable. Commas separate several
destinations in one \c{-h}. Unlike \c{ssh-add}, kageant looks up the
host keys itself, as the rule editor does: in the KiTTY++ host-key store
and, while kageant reads it (\k{pageant-knownhosts}), in OpenSSH's
\c{known_hosts}. A pattern gets \q{Fixed list: host keys known now}.
A destination refused, or a host without a host key, adds no key: the
reason is shown and kageant ends with exit code 1. \c{-h} applies to
SSH-2 key files (PPK) only.

If kageant is already running, the keys go to it with the destinations
as \c{ssh-add -h} sends them (\c{restrict-destination-v00@openssh.com}).
The running kageant keeps the rules with this kageant.exe as their
origin. A key it holds already is sent again: its rules are replaced. Its
policy applies as to any program (\q{Block adding keys over IPC}). The
destination constraint carries no pattern choice: there a pattern rule
is OpenSSH's, matched by the host keys sent. \c{-encrypted} cannot be
used with \c{-h} here: the destinations reach a running kageant with
the decrypted key only.

If kageant starts with \c{-h}, it puts the rules in force itself, in
its rule file: they apply each time the key is loaded, from the startup
list too, until the rule editor or \c{-R} removes them.

For every key the command line adds - with \c{-h} or without - kageant
reports the destination rules the rule file holds for it, as Key details
lists them: \q{main: kageant applies the destination rules stored for
this key:} and the rules on the lines below. A key without rules gets no
such line.

\c{-h} is no longer the short form of \c{-help}: \c{-help} and
\c{\-\-help} show the summary of the options.

\S{pageant-cmdline-remove} Removing keys from a running kageant: \c{-d},
\c{-D}

\I{-d-kageant}\c{-d} removes one key from the running kageant, given
as a key file (its public part is read, no passphrase: a PPK, an SSH-1
key or a public key file) or as its SHA256 fingerprint as the key list
shows it:

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe -d d:\main.ppk -d SHA256:WCvM...

\c{-d} is repeatable. \c{-D} removes every key. Both go to the running
kageant as the requests \c{ssh-add -d} and \c{ssh-add -D} send, so its
policy decides as for any program: \q{Block removing keys over IPC},
the lockdown, the agent lock and the key's destination rules. Without a
running kageant they end with an error. They act before keys on the same
command line are added.

\S{pageant-cmdline-rules} Listing and removing destination rules:
\c{-l}, \c{-R}

\I{-l-kageant}\c{-l} lists every key of the running kageant and of its
rule file, one block per key: the comment, the SHA256 fingerprint,
\q{loaded} or \q{not loaded}, then its destination rules with the origin
of each, as Key details lists them - or \q{any - the key is not
restricted}. Without a running kageant, \c{-l} reads the rule file itself
and starts no agent.

\I{-R-kageant}\c{-R} removes every destination rule of one key from the
rule file, loaded or not, given as a key file or as its SHA256
fingerprint:

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe -R d:\main.ppk

Only a person at the desktop can allow it. kageant shows \q{kageant:
remove destination rules?} with the program behind the request, the key
and its rules, \q{No} the default - or a Windows Hello check instead,
while \q{Confirmations require Windows Hello} is on. Yes removes the
rules: \q{-R: removed the destination rules of d:\\main.ppk.}, exit code
0. No, a refused Windows Hello check, another confirmation already up or
a locked desktop keeps them: \q{-R: not confirmed: the rules stay.}, exit
code 1. A key without rules: \q{-R: d:\\main.ppk has no destination
rules.}, exit code 0. The agent log records the outcome: event 116,
result \c{removed} or \c{refused}, reason \c{cli}. The request reaches
the running kageant as \c{rules-remove-v00@kapper.net} (and \c{-l} as
\c{rules-list-v00@kapper.net}). kageant refuses both on a connection
bound for agent forwarding. Without a running kageant, \c{-R} starts one
and puts up the confirmation itself.

\S{pageant-cmdline-report} Reports and exit codes

From a command prompt or PowerShell, kageant writes its reports to that
window: the results of \c{-h}, \c{-d}, \c{-D}, \c{-R} and \c{-l},
the destination rules of the keys it added, its errors, and \c{-help}.
Its output can be redirected to a file. Started without a console (a
shortcut, the Run box), an error shows a message box. A result shows
nothing then (\c{-l} a message box). The exit code is 1 when an option
is wrong, a \c{-h} destination is refused or a key of it is not added, a
key of \c{-d} or \c{-D} is not removed, or \c{-R} does not remove the
rules. It is 0 otherwise.

\S{pageant-cmdline-command} Making kageant run another program

You can arrange for kageant to start another program once it has
initialised itself and loaded any keys specified on its command
line. This program (perhaps a KiTTY++, or a WinCVS making use of
klink, or whatever) will then be able to use the keys kageant has
loaded.

You do this by specifying the \I{-c-kageant}\c{\-c} option followed
by the command, like this:

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe d:\main.ppk -c C:\KiTTY\kitty.exe

\S{pageant-cmdline-openssh} Integrating with \i{Windows OpenSSH}

Windows's own port of OpenSSH uses the same mechanism as kageant to
talk to its SSH agent (Windows named pipes). This means that Windows
OpenSSH can talk directly to kageant, if it knows where to find
kageant's named pipe.

When kageant starts up, it can optionally write out a file containing
an OpenSSH configuration directive that shows the Windows \c{ssh.exe}
where to find kageant. If you include this file from your Windows SSH
configuration, then \c{ssh.exe} should automatically use kageant as
its agent, so that you can keep your keys in one place and have both
SSH clients able to use them.

The option is \i\c{\-\-openssh\-config}, and you follow it with a filename.

To refer to this file from your main OpenSSH configuration, you can
use the \cq{Include} directive. For example, you might run kageant
like this (with your own username substituted, of course):

\c kageant --openssh-config C:\Users\Simon\.ssh\kageant.conf

and then add a directive like this to your main \cq{.ssh\\config} file
(assuming that lives in the same directory that you just put
\cw{kageant.conf}):

\c Include kageant.conf

\s{ssh-add}: \c{ssh-add} does not read \c{.ssh\\config}, so the
\cw{IdentityAgent} line reaches \c{ssh.exe} only. This also holds for
\q{Register as Windows OpenSSH agent}. \c{ssh-add} finds its agent
through the environment variable \cw{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}. Without it,
\c{ssh-add} talks to the pipe of the Windows \q{OpenSSH Authentication
Agent} service: keys it adds land there, not in kageant. Set the
variable in the PowerShell window that runs \c{ssh-add}:

\c $line = Get-Content "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\kageant.conf"
\c $pipe = $line -replace '^IdentityAgent "(.*)"$','$1'
\c $env:SSH_AUTH_SOCK = $pipe -replace '/','\'

The pipe name changes with each Windows start, so set it again in each
new window. kageant does not set \cw{SSH_AUTH_SOCK} for the user
account: Git for Windows' own \c{ssh} reads the same variable and
cannot use a named pipe.

\s{Note}: this technique only works with \e{Windows's} port of
OpenSSH, which lives at \cw{C:\\Windows\\System32\\OpenSSH\\ssh.exe}
if you have it installed. (If not, it can be installed as a Windows
optional feature, e.g., via Settings > Apps & features > Optional
features > Add a feature > OpenSSH Client.)

There are other versions of OpenSSH for Windows, notably the one that
comes with Windows \cw{git}. Those will likely not work with the same
configuration, because they tend to depend on Unix emulation layers
like MinGW or MSys, so they won't speak Windows native pathname syntax
or understand named pipes. The above instructions will only work with
Windows's own version of OpenSSH.

To use Git for Windows with keys held in kageant, let \cw{git} run
Windows' own \cw{ssh.exe} instead of its own copy:

\b In the Git for Windows installer: on the page \q{Choosing the SSH
executable}, pick \q{Use external OpenSSH}.

\b For an existing installation, run once in PowerShell or a command
prompt:

\lcont{

\c git config --global core.sshCommand C:/Windows/System32/OpenSSH/ssh.exe

Use forward slashes: \cw{git} runs this command through its own shell,
which takes a backslash as an escape character.

}

Then turn on \q{Register as Windows OpenSSH agent} (kageant's tray
menu, or Settings, Agent page). \cw{ssh.exe} finds kageant through the
\cw{IdentityAgent} line. \cw{git} needs no \cw{SSH_AUTH_SOCK}. To check,
list a remote's branches with a key that only kageant holds:

\c git ls-remote git@git.example.com:group/project.git

The branches appear without a passphrase prompt. An environment variable
\cw{GIT_SSH_COMMAND} takes precedence over \c{core.sshCommand}: remove
it, or point it at the same \cw{ssh.exe}. \c{core.sshCommand} in turn
takes precedence over \cw{GIT_SSH}.

Pointing \cw{GIT_SSH} at klink works without this setup: klink finds
kageant on its own. Add \c{git config --global ssh.variant plink}, so
that \cw{git} passes a port (\c{-P}) and \c{--ipv4} / \c{--ipv6}
(\c{-4} / \c{-6}) in klink's form.

\S{pageant-cmdline-unix} Unix-domain sockets: integrating with WSL 1

kageant can listen on the WinSock implementation of \q{Unix-domain
sockets}. These interoperate with the Unix-domain sockets found in the
original Windows Subsystem for Linux (now known as WSL 1). So if you
ask kageant to listen on one of these, then your WSL 1 processes can
talk directly to kageant.

To configure this, run kageant with the option \i\c{\-\-unix}, followed
with a pathname. Then, in WSL 1, set the environment variable
\cw{SSH_AUTH_SOCK} to point at the WSL translation of that pathname.

For example, you might run

\c kageant --unix C:\Users\Simon\.ssh\agent.sock

and in WSL 1, set the environment variable

\c SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/mnt/c/Users/Simon/.ssh/agent.sock

Alternatively, you can add a line to your \cw{.ssh/config} file inside
WSL that reads

\c IdentityAgent /mnt/c/Users/Simon/.ssh/agent.sock

although doing it like that may mean that \cw{ssh-add} commands won't
find the agent, even though \cw{ssh} itself will.

\s{Security note}: Unix-domain sockets are protected against access by
other users by the file protections on their containing directory. So
if your Windows machine is multiuser, make sure you create the socket
inside a directory that other users can't access at all. (In fact,
that's a good idea on general principles.)

\s{Compatibility note}: WSL 2 processes cannot talk to kageant by this
mechanism, because WSL 2's Unix-domain sockets are managed by a
separate Linux kernel, and not by the same kernel that WinSock talks
to.

\S{pageant-cmdline-keylist} Starting with the key list visible

Start kageant with the \i\c{\-\-keylist} option to show the main window
as soon as it starts up.

\S{pageant-cmdline-restrict-acl} Restricting the \i{Windows process ACL}

kageant supports the same \i\c{\-restrict\-acl} option as the other
KiTTY++ utilities to lock down the kageant process's access control;
see \k{using-cmdline-restrict-acl} for why you might want to do this.

By default, if kageant is started with \c{\-restrict\-acl}, it won't
pass this to any KiTTY++ sessions started from its System Tray submenu.
Use \i\c{\-restrict\-kitty\-acl} to change this. (Again, see
\k{using-cmdline-restrict-acl} for details.)

\H{pageant-forward} Using \i{agent forwarding}

Agent forwarding is a mechanism that allows applications on your SSH
server machine to talk to the agent on your client machine.

Note that at present, whether agent forwarding in SSH-2 is available
depends on your server. kageant's protocol is compatible with the
\i{OpenSSH} server, but the \i\cw{ssh.com} server uses a different
agent protocol, which KiTTY++ does not yet support.

To enable agent forwarding, first start kageant. Then set up a KiTTY++
SSH session in which \q{Allow agent forwarding} is enabled (see
\k{config-ssh-agentfwd}). Open the session as normal. (Alternatively,
you can use the \c{\-A} command line option; see
\k{using-cmdline-agent} for details.)

If this has worked, your applications on the server should now have
access to a Unix domain socket which the SSH server will forward
back to KiTTY++, and KiTTY++ will forward on to the agent. To check that
this has actually happened, you can try this command on Unix server
machines:

\c unixbox:~$ echo $SSH_AUTH_SOCK
\c /tmp/ssh-XXNP18Jz/agent.28794
\c unixbox:~$

If the result line comes up blank, agent forwarding has not been
enabled at all.

Now if you run \c{ssh} on the server and use it to connect through
to another server that accepts one of the keys in kageant, you
should be able to log in without a password:

\c unixbox:~$ ssh -v otherunixbox
\c [...]
\c debug: next auth method to try is publickey
\c debug: userauth_pubkey_agent: trying agent key my-kitty-key
\c debug: ssh-userauth2 successful: method publickey
\c [...]

If you enable agent forwarding on \e{that} SSH connection as well
(see the manual for your server-side SSH client to find out how to
do this), your authentication keys will still be available on the
next machine you connect to - two SSH connections away from where
they're actually stored.

In addition, if you have a private key on one of the SSH servers,
you can send it all the way back to kageant using the local
\i\c{ssh-add} command:

\c unixbox:~$ ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_rsa
\c Need passphrase for /home/fred/.ssh/id_rsa
\c Enter passphrase for /home/fred/.ssh/id_rsa:
\c Identity added: /home/fred/.ssh/id_rsa (/home/simon/.ssh/id_rsa)
\c unixbox:~$

and then it's available to every machine that has agent forwarding
available (not just the ones downstream of the place you added it).
This takes \q{Refuse key changes from a forwarded agent} turned off
(\k{pageant-forward-changes}).

\S{pageant-forward-changes} Key changes from a forwarded agent

By default kageant refuses key changes from a forwarded agent: an add
(\c{ssh-add}, also of an encrypted PPK), a removal (\c{ssh-add -d},
\c{-D}), a lock and an unlock (\c{ssh-add -x}, \c{-X}). A program on
the remote server may still list the keys and log in with them. The
setting is \q{Refuse key changes from a forwarded agent (add, remove,
lock)} on the Security page of the settings, \c{blockforwardedchanges}
in the \c{[Agent]} section of kitty.ini (default \c{yes}).

A refused request answers \c{SSH_AGENT_FAILURE}, for an encrypted PPK
(\c{add-ppk@putty.projects.tartarus.org}) \c{SSH_AGENT_EXTENSION_FAILURE}.
The agent log records it twice: the operation's record (110, 114, 115,
120 or 121) with result \c{blocked} and reason \c{forwarded}, and the
refused request (103) with its reason.

kageant recognises a forwarded agent only by the binding the forwarding
client sends: \c{session-bind@openssh.com} with \c{is_forwarding} set.
KiTTY++'s kitty.exe and klink send it, as OpenSSH's \c{ssh} does from
version 8.9. A forwarding client that sends no binding (an older
\c{ssh}, PuTTY) looks to kageant like a program on this computer, and
its changes are not refused.

\H{pageant-destination} The \i{destination} of a key use

klink, kscp, ksftp and kitty.exe bind their agent connection to the
server they connect to (\c{session-bind@openssh.com}). An OpenSSH
client from version 8.9 on - the Windows \c{ssh.exe} included - does
the same. After the key exchange the client sends the server's host
key, the session identifier and the server's signature over that
identifier. kageant verifies the signature and keeps the binding for
the connection. On a bound connection:

\b The key-use confirmation shows \q{Destination:} - the host name and
the SHA256 fingerprint of the server's host key - and one \q{Forwarded
via:} line per agent forwarding hop.

\b The agent log writes \c{dest} (the server the login is for) and
\c{fwd} (the forwarding hops, in the order bound) on every signature,
confirmation and refused request. Events 100, 101 and 103 carry both
as their last two fields.

\b Each binding is a record of its own: kind \c{session-bind}, result
\c{accepted}, \c{dest} and \c{forwarding} (\c{yes} or \c{no}).

The fingerprint is the verified part. The host names are looked up in
the KiTTY++ host-key store and, while kageant reads it
(\k{pageant-knownhosts}), in OpenSSH's \c{known_hosts} (plain entries: a
hashed entry holds no name). A name from \c{known_hosts} is marked
\c{(known_hosts)}. The names are shown for orientation only. klink, kscp,
ksftp and kitty.exe also send the name
they connected to, right after the binding
(\c{session-bind-host-v00@kapper.net}). That name comes first, marked
\c{(client)}: it is the client's word, never verified and never used
for a decision. The port sent with it is used for one decision only: a
port condition of an \q{All servers of a host CA} destination
(\k{pageant-restrict-hostca}). The names the lookup finds follow when they differ from
it, at most 5 names in all, then \c{+N} for the rest:
\c{SHA256:WCvM... monitor.example.net (client) 192.0.2.21 +1}. An
OpenSSH client sends no name: the lookup's names stand alone. Without a
name the fingerprint stands alone. The binding's own record is written
before the client's name arrives and carries the lookup's names only.

A binding whose signature does not verify is refused and logged as a
refused request with the reason. The login goes on. A connection takes
up to 16 bindings, a session identifier once, and none after a binding
for a login.

A KiTTY++ login holds one agent connection: the binding first, then
the key list and every signature. With agent forwarding, kitty.exe and
klink bind each forwarded agent connection for forwarding
(\c{forwarding} \c{yes}) before they relay it. A jump host's login
binds a connection of its own to the jump host. The Terminal Window's
Event Log shows the outcome: \q{Agent connection bound to host key
...}, or \q{Agent connection not bound} with the reason. An agent
without \c{session-bind@openssh.com} (PuTTY's Pageant, an older
kageant) answers a failure. The login then goes on unbound. A GSSAPI
key exchange has no host key signature: such a login is not bound.
On a forwarded agent connection the name sent after the binding is the
name of the server whose connection it is. kageant's own extensions
are described in full in
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}{docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}.

\S{pageant-knownhosts} OpenSSH's \c{known_hosts}

kageant reads \c{%USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\known_hosts} only while
\q{Use OpenSSH's known_hosts as a host-key source} is ticked (Settings,
\q{Agent} page, \c{[Agent] readknownhosts=yes} in kitty.ini). It is off
by default: the file is OpenSSH's. Nobody set it as a source for
kageant. PuTTY never reads it. KiTTY++ never writes it. Off, kageant
does not open the file at all. On, the file serves three uses:

\b Host names: \c{dest} and \c{fwd} in the agent log and the key-use
confirmation. A name from the file is marked:
\c{SHA256:... web07.example.com (known_hosts)}.

\b \q{Look up host keys} in the rule editor: plain, hashed and
\c{@cert-authority} entries, listed with the source \c{known_hosts} or
\c{known_hosts, @cert-authority}.

\b \q{Checked at each login}: a name from the file counts
as a stored name. A signature such a rule permitted only through a name
from the file carries the reason \c{known_hosts} in its record (event
100).

A rule a program set (\c{ssh-add -h}) also shows whether its host keys
are the ones the file holds for its host name (\k{pageant-restrict}).

OK with the setting newly ticked checks the file at once. The box shows
it not there yet (ssh.exe creates it with the first host key it
stores), not readable (with Windows' reason), or read with its number
of host entries and of hashed ones.

A hashed entry (\c{|1|...}, written while OpenSSH's
\c{HashKnownHosts} is \c{yes}) holds no host name. A pattern
destination (\q{Checked at each login: any matching host}) never matches such a
server. To store the names again:

\b Set \c{HashKnownHosts no} in \c{%USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\config}, so new
entries carry names.

\b For every server already hashed run \c{ssh-keygen -R <host>} (it
removes the hashed line too), then connect once with ssh.exe and accept
the key again.

OpenSSH cannot turn a hashed line back into a name.

The check and its warning come only from OK in Settings. With
\c{readknownhosts=yes} written into kitty.ini directly, kageant reads
the file without either: hashed entries then give no name without
notice.

With destinations \q{Fixed list: host keys known now} in the rule
store, the same box offers to look their host keys up again,
\c{known_hosts} included. Yes adds the keys found to each such
destination and keeps the ones it holds. Those destinations then admit
more servers: No is the default. A pattern wider than the editor takes
is left as it is. The rule file is saved at once. Each key whose
destinations changed gets an agent log record (event 104, result
\c{keys-added}, reason \c{known_hosts}).

\q{Register as Windows OpenSSH agent} with this setting off adds a
hint to its confirmation: pattern destinations with ssh.exe need it,
since ssh.exe stores its host keys in \c{known_hosts} only.

A key without destination rules behaves as before: kageant refuses
nothing because of the destination. A connection without a binding -
PuTTY, older KiTTY++ versions and OpenSSH clients before 8.9 - gets the
confirmation as before. A key restricted to destinations is used only
where its rules permit (\k{pageant-restrict}).

\H{pageant-restrict} Restricting a key to \i{destinations}

A key can be restricted to the servers it may log in to. kageant then
signs a login with that key only on a connection bound to one of those
servers (\k{pageant-destination}). It is OpenSSH's destination
constraint, with the same rules: what \c{ssh-add -h} sets in OpenSSH's
agent, kageant honours too.

\b \e{From the key list}: double-click the key, then \q{Restrict...}
beside \q{Destinations:}. The rule editor shows every key with rules,
each with its destinations below it and their attributes below those.
\q{Add destination} offers two kinds. \q{Server or pattern}: the host
name (\c{host:port} for another port than 22), a user (optional: the
key then logs in as that user only, \c{*} and \c{?} as wildcards), a
via host (optional: the key is used through an agent forwarded to that
host, towards this one) and \q{Allow agent forwarding to the other
destinations}. \q{Look up host keys} finds the host's keys in the
KiTTY++ host-key store, in the CA store and, while kageant reads it
(\k{pageant-knownhosts}), in \c{known_hosts} (hashed and
\c{@cert-authority} entries included). It lists them with where each
was found. A host without a stored key cannot be added: log in to it
once first. \q{All servers of a host CA}: a CA of the KiTTY++ CA store
under \q{CA:}, no host name, no host key in the rule
(\k{pageant-restrict-hostca}). User, via host and forwarding work as for
a server. OK saves the rules and puts them in force at once.

\b \e{With OpenSSH's ssh-add}: \c{ssh-add -h web01 key}, or
\c{ssh-add -h jump01 -h "jump01>web01" key} for a jump host. ssh-add
looks the host keys up in OpenSSH's \c{known_hosts} itself. Set
\cw{SSH_AUTH_SOCK} first (\k{pageant-cmdline-openssh}).

\b \e{With kageant's own -h}: \c{kageant -h web01 key.ppk}, the same
values, the host keys looked up as the rule editor looks them up
(\k{pageant-cmdline-restrict}).

The rules of a key stay in the rule file when the key is removed, and
apply again when it is loaded. kageant shows it: the notice of a key added
by a program (\c{ssh-add}, kageant's command line, an encrypted PPK) or
through Add Key or drag and drop ends in \q{Destination rules stored for
main apply: web01, jump01 > web02.} (the first three destinations, then
\c{+N}). The notice of a removal by a program, and a notice of its own
for Remove and Remove All in the key list, read \q{The destination rules
of main stay stored and apply again when it is loaded. The rule editor
(Key details > Restrict...) removes them.} - for several keys \q{3
removed keys keep their destination rules: ...}. Both follow \q{Notify
when a key is used}. A key loaded from the startup list at kageant's
start gets no such notice (it would show at every Windows start): the key
list marks it \q{restricted}. The rule editor, or \c{kageant -R}
(\k{pageant-cmdline-rules}), removes the rules.

A host typed with a port (\c{web01:2222}, \c{[web01]:2222}) keeps it:
the tree and Key details show it. A later look-up (\q{Look up host
keys}, the offer to look up again) searches that port. The login is
decided by the host keys alone, as before. A rule from \c{ssh-add -h}
holds no port.

A re-add with \c{-h} replaces the key's rules. A re-add without \c{-h}
keeps them: rules are lifted in the rule editor (\q{Remove}), not by a
program that holds the private key. The limits are OpenSSH's: 1024
destinations per key, a user name of 256 bytes.

In the rule editor a host name may be a pattern: \c{*} and \c{?}, where
\c{*} also covers dots (\c{*.customer.com} takes
\c{a.b.customer.com}). The editor takes a pattern only with two
labels without a wildcard at its end and the wildcard in a label
before them: \c{*.customer.com} or \c{web*.customer.com}, not
\c{*}, \c{*.com} or \c{customer.*}. \q{Pattern:} under \q{Host:}
then decides how a server is matched:

\b \q{Fixed list: host keys known now} (set for a new pattern):
\q{Look up host keys} takes every key stored under a name the pattern
matches, in the KiTTY++ host-key store and, while kageant reads it, in
\c{known_hosts} (plain entries), as a snapshot. A server added later
needs another look-up.

\b \q{Checked at each login: any matching host}: at each login kageant matches the
pattern against the names the host-key store holds for the server's host
key (and \c{known_hosts}, while kageant reads it). A new server works
after its first host key prompt. The editor shows the trade-off under
the choice: a program running as this Windows user that writes the
host-key store, or \c{known_hosts} while kageant reads it, can widen the
rule. The name a client sends (\c{session-bind-host-v00@kapper.net}) is
never used for it.

The former choice \q{Host CA only} is gone: \q{All servers of a host CA}
takes its place (\k{pageant-restrict-hostca}). A rule file that still
holds it loads. Such a destination matches no server. Key details marks
it \q{Host CA only: no longer offered, matches no server}. The editor
refuses OK until it gets one of the two choices or is removed.

\c{ssh-add -h} stays as in OpenSSH: there a pattern finds
\c{@cert-authority} lines only. Key details and the editor's tree show
the choice.

Each rule keeps its origin. The editor's tree and Key details show it
under the destination:

\b \q{Set by C:\\Windows\\System32\\OpenSSH\\ssh-add.exe, 2026-10-30
14:02 (host keys looked up by that program)}: a rule a program set over
the pipe (\c{ssh-add -h}), with the program kageant found behind the
request and the time. While kageant reads \c{known_hosts}, the line goes
on with \q{host keys as in known_hosts}, or \q{host keys not in
known_hosts: 1 of 2} when the program sent host keys the file does not
hold for the rule's host name.

\b \q{Set in the rule editor}: a rule made or changed in the editor.

\b \q{Origin not recorded}: a rule from a rule file of an older
version.

The rule file keeps the choices and the origins in its third format.
Files of the first and second formats still load, their rules with
\q{Origin not recorded}.

On a connection bound for a login kageant lists the keys in three
groups, each in the offer order: keys whose rules match this server by
its host keys (a CA certificate for the host name as written included),
then keys whose rules match it by a pattern only, then the keys without
rules. A connection without a binding keeps the offer order.

The rules follow the key by its fingerprint, however the key reaches
kageant: from the startup list, the key list or a program. They are
kept in one file, \c{kageant-rules.dat}: in \c{%APPDATA%\\KiTTY}, or
beside kitty.ini in a portable copy, encrypted with DPAPI for the
Windows account. kageant reads it in full at the start and writes it in
full through a temporary file. With \c{readonly=yes} in kitty.ini the
rules apply until kageant stops. Rules stay when their key is removed:
the rule editor lists such a key as \q{not loaded}.

Key details lists the rules under \q{Destinations:}: the count on the
first line (\q{2 destinations - the key signs a login to these servers
only}), then one line per destination - the host, then \q{user},
\q{via} and \q{forwarding} where the destination has them
(\c{web01.example.com  user deploy  via jump.example.com  forwarding}),
a CA destination as \c{CA: }\e{name}\c{ (Valid hosts: }\e{expression}\c{)}
- each with its origin on the line below it. A key without rules shows
\q{any - the key is not restricted}. The list
takes the height the window grows by. The key list adds \q{restricted}
to the key's state and draws the row in the theme's info colour (blue).
A key not loaded stays grey. The flash of a key use comes first. A
high-contrast theme keeps the system colours.

On a connection, a restricted key is checked as OpenSSH's agent checks
it:

\b Without a binding the key is listed (local use) and every signature
is refused. A connection whose binding was refused gets neither.

\b On a bound connection the key is listed only when the rules permit
the servers bound - each hop from the one before it, the first from
this computer. A server never learns of a key that may not go to it.

\b A signature needs the data of a login (a \q{publickey} request)
for the session last bound, as the user of the rule. A key may not sign
on a connection bound for agent forwarding. With more than one binding
the client must name the server's host key in the request (OpenSSH
does).

\b A host certificate is accepted for a CA key of the rule when the
certificate is valid for the rule's host name. A certificate without
names is valid for no host, as in OpenSSH 10.3
(\k{pageant-restrict-hostca}).

\b A program may not remove a restricted key through a connection the
key may not be used on.

A client that sees fewer keys moves on to the next method. Nothing in
the list explains why. So klink, kscp, ksftp and kitty.exe query kageant
after the binding for the number of keys it held back on this connection
(\c{withheld-keys-v00@kapper.net}). When that count is not 0, they show
before the next authentication method, in the terminal or on stderr and
in the Terminal Window's Event Log:

\c kageant: 1 key restricted to other servers was not offered.

kageant gives the count only on a connection bound for a login, never on
one bound for agent forwarding: a remote server must not learn that keys
for other servers exist. PuTTY's Pageant and OpenSSH's agent answer the
query with a failure: nothing is shown. The wire format is in
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}{docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}.

When the key list a program receives is empty because the rules left out
every key kageant holds, kageant shows a notice, once per agent
connection: the program, the server of the connection and the reason.
For example:

\c ssh.exe got no key for web01.
\c Every key kageant holds is restricted to other servers.

The other reasons: the connection is bound for agent forwarding
(\q{No key kageant holds may be used through this forwarded
connection.}), its session-bind was refused (the server was not
verified), or the rule file is damaged. The notice follows
\q{Notify when a key is used}. The agent log records it whatever that
setting: kind \c{restricted}, result \c{no-key-offered}, the reason as a
token (\c{destination}, \c{forwarding-hop}, \c{bind-failed},
\c{store-damaged}), \c{dest} and \c{fwd}: event ID 104. A connection
without a session-bind lists the restricted keys (local use): it never
gets this notice.

Each refusal is a record of its own: kind \c{restricted}, result
\c{sign-refused} or \c{remove-refused}, the reason as a token
(\c{unbound}, \c{bind-failed}, \c{not-userauth}, \c{destination},
\c{forwarding-hop}, \c{session-id}, \c{hostkey}, \c{no-port}), the binding's
\c{dest} and \c{fwd} and the key's rules in \c{rule}: event ID 104, a
warning. A key added with rules carries them in event 110's
\c{restrict} field.

A rule file that cannot be read, decrypted or checked is never taken as
\q{no rules}: every key is refused for signing over the pipe (reason
\c{store-damaged}). A notice shows it. Event 104 records the file and
why. \c{ssh-add -h} does not write over that file. Saving rules in
the rule editor keeps the old file as
\c{kageant-rules.dat.damaged-}\e{date}\c{-}\e{time} and writes the
rules shown.

\S{pageant-restrict-hostca} All servers of a host CA

A host CA (\i{certificate authority}) signs the host keys of a fleet of
servers. Each server holds a host certificate: its host key, the names
it is valid for, a validity period and the CA's signature. The server
sends its certificate in the key exchange. The client side holds only
the CA's public key.

Setting up the CA:

\b Create the CA and sign the servers' host keys with their names as in
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/SSH-CERTIFICATES.md}{docs/SSH-CERTIFICATES.md}
(\c{ssh-keygen -h -n web01.example.com}, \c{HostCertificate} in
\c{sshd_config}).

\b Enter the CA's public key in the KiTTY++ CA store: the configuration
box, \q{Application} > \q{Security} > \q{Certificate Authorities}
(\k{kitty-cfg-host-cas}), or \c{kitty.exe -host-ca} for the same editor
in a window of its own. Fill in \q{Name for this CA (shown in log
messages)}, \q{Public key of certification authority} (or \q{Read from
file}) and \q{Valid hosts this key is trusted to certify}
(\c{*.example.com}), then \q{Save}.

A key for every server of that CA:

\n Key details > \q{Restrict...} > \q{Add destination} > \q{All servers
of a host CA}.

\n \q{CA:} pick the CA. \q{Host keys:} shows its key
(\c{CA ssh-ed25519 255 SHA256:...   (CA store: }\e{name}\c{)}) and its
\q{Valid hosts}. Under the CA the editor warns: a program running as
this Windows user that edits the CA store can widen the rule.

\n OK. The rule is in force at once.

At each login kageant reads the CA from the CA store: its public key and
its \q{Valid hosts}. A change there applies at once. kageant signs a
login to a server whose host certificate is signed by that public key,
is valid now and holds a name \q{Valid hosts} admits.

A port condition in \q{Valid hosts} is checked against the port the
client sends after its session-bind
(\c{session-bind-host-v00@kapper.net}). KiTTY++'s kitty.exe, klink, kscp
and ksftp send it. \c{*.example.com && port:2222} then admits a login to
\c{web01.example.com} on port 2222 only. The port is the client's word:
kageant cannot verify it. A client that sends no port (OpenSSH's
\c{ssh.exe}) meets no port condition, negated or not.
\c{*.example.com && port:22} admits no server,
\c{*.example.com && !port:22} none either, \c{*.example.com || port:22}
admits every name in \c{example.com}. For such clients the editor
offers \q{Ignore the port for clients that send none} under the CA,
shown while the CA's \q{Valid hosts} has a port condition. With it the
port conditions are dropped from the expression for a client that sends
no port: \c{*.example.com && port:2222} and
\c{*.example.com || port:22} admit every name in \c{example.com}.
\c{*.example.org || port:22} admits no name outside \c{example.org}. An
expression of port conditions only admits nothing. A client that sends a
port is always checked against that port. No signature for:

\b a server with a plain host key (no certificate),

\b a certificate from another CA, or one whose CA signature is RSA
with SHA-1,

\b an expired certificate, or one not yet valid,

\b a certificate whose names \q{Valid hosts} does not admit,

\b a login that \q{Valid hosts} admits only with a port the client did
not send (reason \c{no-port} in event 104),

\b a certificate without names (below),

\b any server, when the CA is gone from the CA store: Key details shows
\q{CA: }\e{name}\q{ (not in the CA store: matches no server)}.

Key details and the editor's tree show the destination as
\c{CA: }\e{name}\c{ (Valid hosts: }\e{expression}\c{)}, read from the CA
store when shown. With \q{Ignore the port for clients that send none}
\c{, port ignored for clients that send none} follows.

\c{ssh-add -h} has no pattern choice. OpenSSH treats the certificate's
name as the pattern and the rule's name as the text:
\c{ssh-add -h "*.example.com"} does not cover \c{web.example.com}. With
\c{ssh-add -h} list each server:
\c{ssh-add -h web.example.com -h srv01.example.com key}. kageant checks \c{ssh-add -h} rules with PuTTY's certificate
check: the CA, the validity period and the signature, then the names.
The rule's host name must equal one of the certificate's names exactly,
character for character. A certificate without names is valid for no
host.

\e{Certificates without names.} A host certificate signed without
\c{-n} holds no names. OpenSSH up to 10.2 took it as valid for every
host. OpenSSH 10.3 matches it against no host (its release notes:
\W{https://openssh.org/txt/release-10.3}{openssh.org/txt/release-10.3}).
KiTTY++ follows OpenSSH 10.3 in kageant's rules as in its clients
(\k{config-ssh-cert-nameless}). A CA can issue such a certificate by
accident. A stolen one lets one server pose as any host. The fix: sign
the host keys with their names (\c{-n}). The exception: \q{Accept
certificates without names (valid for every host in Valid hosts)} in the
CA's record. With it, a destination \q{All servers of a host CA} takes
that CA's certificates without names too. \s{Not recommended}: the box
reopens the gap OpenSSH 10.3 closed. It applies to the CA store's CAs
only, never to an \c{ssh-add -h} rule.

Which kind to take:

\b \q{All servers of a host CA}: a fleet whose host keys a CA signs. A
new server passes as soon as it holds a certificate with its name.
Nothing changes on this computer.

\b \q{Fixed list: host keys known now}: a known set of servers
without a CA. A new server needs another \q{Look up host keys}.

\b \q{Checked at each login: any matching host}: customer servers without a CA, where
new ones appear. A new server passes after its first host key prompt.
The editor warns: a program running as this Windows user that writes
the host-key store, or \c{known_hosts} while kageant reads it, can widen
the rule.

\H{pageant-deferred-decryption} Loading keys without decrypting them

You can add keys to kageant \e{without} decrypting them. The key
file will be held in kageant's memory still encrypted, and when a
client program first tries to use the key, kageant will display a
dialog box prompting for the passphrase so that the key can be
decrypted.

This works the same way whether the key is used by an instance of
KiTTY++ running locally, or a remote client connecting to kageant
through agent forwarding.

To add a key to kageant in this encrypted form, press the \q{Add Key
(encrypted)} button in the kageant main window. kageant will bring up a file dialog, in
just the same way as it would for the plain \q{Add Key} button. But it
won't prompt for a passphrase. Instead, the key will be listed in the
main window with \q{(encrypted)} after it.

To start kageant up in the first place with encrypted keys loaded into
it, you can use the \i\c{\-\-encrypted} option on the command line. For
example:

\c C:\KiTTY\kageant.exe --encrypted d:\main.ppk

After a key has been decrypted for the first use, it remains
decrypted, so that it can be used again. The main window will list
the key with \q{(\i{re-encryptable})} after it. You can revert it
to the previous state, where a passphrase is required, using the
\q{\i{Re-encrypt}} button in the kageant main window.

You can also \q{re-encrypt} all keys that were added encrypted:
right-click the key list in the main window and choose \q{Re-encrypt
All Keys}.
(Note that this does \e{not} discard cleartext keys that were not
previously added encrypted!)

Re-encrypting is done in kageant's own window only. kageant does not
serve PuTTY's \c{reencrypt} and \c{reencrypt-all} agent extensions to
other programs: a remote host behind a forwarded agent must not
re-encrypt your keys. PuTTY's \c{list-extended} is not served to programs
either. They read the state of a key with kageant's
\c{key-state-v00@kapper.net}. To a program the three are unknown names.
\c{query} does not list them. Details in
\W{https://github.com/hknet/KiTTY/blob/kitty-0.85/docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}{docs/PROTOCOL-AGENT-KAPPER.md}.

\s{Note}: kageant brings the prompt to the front with the keyboard
focus. Check that the prompt window is active before typing the
passphrase: if Windows refused the switch, the typing goes to the window
in front.

\H{pageant-autoencrypt} Re-encrypting keys after idle

A decrypted key stays usable until kageant stops. \q{Re-encrypt keys
after idle}, on the Security page of the settings, puts a key back into
its encrypted state once the given time has passed since it was
decrypted or last signed, whichever is later; the next use prompts for
the passphrase and the clock starts again. kageant
keeps the encrypted copy in memory, so nothing is read from disk and
the key stays listed. Only keys that have a passphrase take part.

The setting has a mode and a time. \q{Off} runs no agent timer; a key
can still carry its own time in its key details. \q{Default for keys
without their own setting} applies the time to every key that has no
value of its own, and a key's own value wins for that key. \q{Enforced
for every key} applies the time to every key and ignores the per-key
values, for a site or a network whose rules demand it; key details
then show the field greyed with the enforced value, and the tray tip
reports this.

The time is seconds, or a number with \c{m}, \c{h} or \c{d} (\c{10m},
\c{2h}, \c{1d}), from 30 seconds to 7 days - or \c{immediately}: right
after each signature, so every use costs the passphrase; a login that
signs twice in one handshake then prompts twice. Both fields are combo
boxes listing these values; any other time can be typed. The stored
word is \c{use}, which \c{immediately} means as well. In \c{kitty.ini}
these are \c{autoencryptmode} and \c{autoencryptseconds} in the
\c{[Agent]} section; a key's own value travels on its startup entry.

\H{pageant-lock} Locking the agent

A \i{locked agent} keeps its keys but neither offers nor uses them: a
client requesting the key list gets an empty one and falls back to its
other ways of logging in, and a request to sign, add or remove a key is
refused. The key list shows every key as \q{locked}, and the tray tip
reads \q{agent LOCKED}. The lock is a gate, not encryption: it stops
programs from using the agent while it lasts, and adds nothing against
someone who can read kageant's memory.

\q{Lock agent} in the tray menu locks it; the same entry then reads
\q{Unlock agent} and unlocks it again, whoever locked it.

The lock lasts while kageant runs; a restart starts it unlocked, as
OpenSSH's agent does.

\q{When Windows locks}, on the Security page of the settings, ties the
agent to the Windows session. \q{Keep the agent working} (the default)
leaves it alone, so a job that uses the agent keeps running while the
screen is locked. \q{Lock the agent} locks it when Windows locks or a
remote desktop disconnects, and unlocks it when Windows unlocks.
\q{Re-encrypt the keys} also puts every key that has a passphrase back
into its encrypted state; when Windows unlocks, kageant prompts for the
passphrase of each key it re-encrypted that has a key file to read it
from, and the others prompt on first use. In \c{kitty.ini} this is
\c{lockwithwindows} (\c{keep}, \c{lock} or \c{reencrypt}) in the
\c{[Agent]} section.

\c{ssh-add -x} and \c{ssh-add -X} lock and unlock the agent with a
passphrase of the program's choosing - but only with \q{Allow locking
the agent over IPC} ticked on the Security page (\c{allowipclock=yes})
and, over a forwarded agent, \q{Refuse key changes from a forwarded
agent} turned off (\k{pageant-forward-changes});
otherwise they are refused and the agent log notes it. A program's lock
raises a notice identifying the program, and the tray menu's \q{Unlock agent}
clears it without the passphrase. After each wrong passphrase the next
unlock is refused for 0.1 s per wrong passphrase so far, at most 10 s.
After the tenth wrong passphrase programs can no longer unlock the agent,
the right passphrase included, until \q{Unlock agent} in the tray menu; a
notice identifies the program, and the keys stay loaded.

\H{pageant-security} Security considerations

\I{security risk}Using kageant for public-key authentication gives you the
convenience of being able to open multiple SSH sessions without
having to type a passphrase every time, but also gives you the
security benefit of never storing a decrypted private key on disk.
Many people feel this is a good compromise between security and
convenience.

It \e{is} a compromise, however. Holding your decrypted private keys
in kageant is better than storing them in easy-to-find disk files,
but still less secure than not storing them anywhere at all. This is
for two reasons:

\b Windows unfortunately provides no way to protect pieces of memory
from being written to the system \i{swap file}. So if kageant is holding
your private keys for a long period of time, it's possible that
decrypted private key data may be written to the system swap file,
and an attacker who gained access to your hard disk later on might
be able to recover that data. (However, if you stored an unencrypted
key in a disk file they would \e{certainly} be able to recover it.)

\b Although, like most modern operating systems, Windows prevents
programs from accidentally accessing one another's memory space, it
does allow programs to access one another's memory space
deliberately, for special purposes such as debugging. This means
that if you allow a virus, trojan, or other malicious program on to
your Windows system while kageant is running, it could access the
memory of the kageant process, extract your decrypted authentication
keys, and send them back to its master.

Similarly, use of agent \e{forwarding} is a security improvement on
other methods of one-touch authentication, but not perfect. Holding
your keys in kageant on your Windows box has a security advantage
over holding them on the remote server machine itself (either in an
agent or just unencrypted on disk), because if the server machine
ever sees your unencrypted private key then the sysadmin or anyone
who cracks the machine can steal the keys and pretend to be you for
as long as they want.

However, the sysadmin of the server machine can always pretend to be
you \e{on that machine}. So if you forward your agent to a server
machine, then the sysadmin of that machine can access the forwarded
agent connection and request signatures from any of your private keys,
and can therefore log in to other machines as you. They can only do
this to a limited extent - when the agent forwarding disappears they
lose the ability - but using kageant doesn't actually \e{prevent} the
sysadmin (or hackers) on the server from doing this.

Therefore, if you don't trust the sysadmin of a server machine, you
should \e{never} use agent forwarding to that machine. (Of course
you also shouldn't store private keys on that machine, type
passphrases into it, or log into other machines from it in any way
at all; kageant is hardly unique in this respect.)
